<article class="env-corollary" id="0552">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0552">Corollary <span data-tag="0552">3.5.7.15</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of Kan complexes which is $n$-connective for some integer $n$. Then the induced map $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(f): \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(X) \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(Y)$ is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram of Kan complexes 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f} &amp;  \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(X) \ar [d]^{\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(f) } \\ Y \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(Y), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the horizontal maps are $n$-connective (Remark <a data-tag="052M" href="/tag/052M">3.5.3.22</a>). Applying Corollary <a data-tag="04H9" href="/tag/04H9">3.5.1.28</a>, we deduce that $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(f)$ is $n$-connective. Since $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(X)$ and $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(Y)$ are $(n-1)$-truncated (Example <a data-tag="054Q" href="/tag/054Q">3.5.7.2</a>), Corollary <a data-tag="0551" href="/tag/0551">3.5.7.14</a> guarantees that $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(f)$ is a homotopy equivalence. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
